INT. EMBASSY STAIRWELL - NIGHT

Concrete. Fluorescent tubes buzzing. A single door marked EXIT.

DELPHINE VOSKUIJLEN, 30s, trench coat, wet from rain, takes the stairs two at a time. A manila envelope pressed to her ribs.

A GUARD blocks the landing. Mid-forties, crew cut, hand on a holstered radio.

GUARD
Ma'am. This level is closed.

DELPHINE
I have a pass.

GUARD
Not for this level.

She slows. Looks him up and down. Steps closer.

DELPHINE
You're not on the roster.

GUARD
Neither are you.

She lets out a short laugh. He doesn't smile.

DELPHINE
Who sent you?

GUARD
Nobody sends me. I come when I'm needed.

DELPHINE
What do I call you?

He considers. Behind him, the elevator DINGS. Footsteps echo on the floor above.

GUARD
Nasir.

DELPHINE
Nasir. I need to get to the ninth floor.

NASIR
The ninth floor doesn't exist.

DELPHINE
Then I'll take the stairs that go to it.

NASIR
Ms. Voskuijlen, I read your file. I know who you are and what you're carrying. I also know who's waiting upstairs.

DELPHINE
Then step aside.

NASIR
I can't.

DELPHINE
Why?

NASIR
Because if you go up, he kills you. And if I let you go, he kills me.

DELPHINE
Then we're both dead.

NASIR
Unless you give me the envelope.

She holds it tighter. He holds out an open palm. Calm. Almost kind.

NASIR (CONT'D)
Give it to me. Walk down. Leave the country by dawn. I will deliver it myself.

DELPHINE
Why would I trust you?

NASIR
Because there's a third man behind you on the stairs. And he is not here to talk.

She doesn't turn. Her shoulders stiffen. Below, a boot scuffs concrete.

DELPHINE
(whispering)
Thaddeus.

NASIR
Yes.

THADDEUS, 50s, silver hair, bespoke suit, stands two flights down, hands in pockets. He smiles up at them.

THADDEUS
Delphine. Don't be dramatic.

She looks at Nasir. At the envelope. At the open palm.

She hands it over.
